\nWait \u2014 but the function under test references `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name` via the module attribute `pygments.lexers`, so monkeypatching `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name` works since the code does `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)` \u2014 attribute lookup at call time. Yes.\n\nHmm, but careful: `pygments.lexers` is a module (`pygments.lexers` is `pygments/lexers/__init__.py`). The code does `import pygments.lexer` and `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype` \u2014 wait, the snippet shows `import pygments.lexer` but uses `pygments.lexers...`. The actual httpie code has `import pygments.lexers`. The snippet's first line `import pygments.lexer` is probably a typo in the extraction. Anyway, `pygments.lexers` must be imported in the module. Since the code calls `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype`, the module must import pygments.lexers.\n\nSo monkeypatching `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name` affects the code under test. Good.\n\nSimilarly, I could spy on get_lexer_for_mimetype to verify the loop tries candidates in order. Maybe overkill. But the spy test for for-else is valuable for branch coverage: the `else` clause of the for loop is a branch; both outcomes covered by tests (break vs. fall-through). The spy test confirms break happened (name lookup not called). Actually branch coverage of for-else is already achieved by having both a breaking case and a non-breaking case. The spy just adds behavioral assertion. I'll include it \u2014 it's good TDD practice.\n\nAlso, I might add a spy to verify that for 'application/vnd.foo+json', the mimetype loop tried 'application/vnd.foo+json' and 'application/vnd.foo' (both ClassNotFound) before 'application/json'. That verifies lines 122-124 built the list correctly. Let me add:\n\n```python\ndef test_plus_subtype_tries_derived_mimetypes(monkeypatch):\n    tried = []\n    real = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype\n\n    def spy(mime, **options):\n        tried.append(mime)\n        return real(mime, **options)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_for_mimetype', spy)\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+json')\n    assert tried == ['application/vnd.foo+json', 'application/vnd.foo', 'application/json']\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n```\n\nThat's a nice assertion of lines 120-124.\n\nAnd for name loop order:\n\n```python\ndef test_name_lookup_tries_candidates_in_order(monkeypatch):\n    tried = []\n    real = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name\n\n    def spy(name, **options):\n        tried.append(name)\n        try:\n            return real(name, **options)\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            raise\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_by_name', spy)\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar+python')\n    assert tried == ['bar', 'python']\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('python')\n```\n\nHmm, the spy wrapper is pointless there; just append and call real. Fine.\n\nAlso for the 'json' in subtype line 129-130: 'application/vnd.foo+json' has 'json' in subtype \u2192 names get 'json' appended. I can spy and check names tried: for 'application/vnd.foo+json', mimetype lookup succeeds at 'application/json' so name loop not reached. To check the 'json' append, use a mime where mimetype lookups all fail but 'json' name is appended: e.g., 'foo/bar+json': subtype='bar+json', names=['bar', 'json', 'json'], mime_types=['foo/bar+json', 'foo/bar', 'foo/json'] \u2014 none match. Name loop: 'bar' fails, 'json' succeeds \u2192 JsonLexer. That covers line 130's effect and the name loop with mixed success/failure. Let me add:\n\n```python\ndef test_json_in_subtype_falls_back_to_json_lexer_name(monkeypatch):\n    tried = []\n    ...\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar+json')\n    assert tried == ['bar', 'json', 'json']\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n```\n\nWait, names: subtype_name='bar', subtype_suffix='json' \u2192 lexer_names = ['bar', 'json']; then 'json' in subtype \u2192 append 'json' \u2192 ['bar', 'json', 'json']. Yes, tried == ['bar', 'json', 'json'].\n\nHmm, but is this over-testing implementation details? The task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" with assertions verifying postconditions. Spying on call order is fine and improves confidence. But it does couple to implementation. I think it's acceptable and even desirable here for branch coverage verification. But maybe keep the spies minimal \u2014 I'll include a couple.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the primary goal is line/branch coverage of lines 115-156. Pure behavior tests already cover all lines and branches as analyzed. The spy tests add robustness. I'll include them but keep them clean.\n\nOne more branch: line 117 `if '+' not in subtype` \u2014 both covered. Line 129 \u2014 both covered. Line 134 loop: break and exhaust \u2014 covered. Line 141 loop: with success and all-fail \u2014 covered. Line 147: all short-circuit and full-evaluation paths \u2014 covered. Line 149 try: success and ValueError \u2014 covered.\n\nWait, line 147 condition `(not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer))`:\n- `not lexer` True, isinstance not evaluated (short-circuit) \u2014 test 8/9.\n- `not lexer` False, isinstance True \u2014 test 6/7.\n- `not lexer` False, isinstance False \u2014 test 10.\n- explicit_json False \u2014 test 12 (short-circuit at first operand).\n- body empty \u2014 test 11 (short-circuit at second operand).\n\nAll branch outcomes covered.\n\nNow, is `lexer` returned by `get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/plain')` an instance of TextLexer? Yes.\n\nLet me also double check `get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')`: lexer is None \u2192 `not lexer` True \u2192 body truthy \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 lexer = get_lexer_by_name('json') \u2192 JsonLexer. Good.\n\nAnd `get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='not json')`: json.loads('not json') raises ValueError \u2192 pass \u2192 return None. Good.\n\nHmm wait, json.loads('not json') \u2014 raises ValueError (json.JSONDecodeError subclasses ValueError). Good.\n\nWhat about body='\"just a string\"'? json.loads('\"just a string\"') succeeds \u2192 JSON lexer. Not needed.\n\nNow, the module path: `httpie.output.formatters.colors`. Let me verify with get_info('get_lexer'). Also maybe get_info('httpie.output.formatters.colors.get_lexer')? The tool takes \"class, function or method name, as in 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C.\" So probably just 'get_lexer'. Let me call it.\n\nAlso, I want to verify pygments lexer names/mimetypes available. get_info might not help for third-party. I'll rely on knowledge but make assertions robust: instead of asserting exact class equality via get_lexer_by_name (which is robust), that's fine.\n\nActually, one risk: `type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')` \u2014 get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/json') returns JsonLexer instance; get_lexer_by_name('json') returns JsonLexer instance. Same class. Good.\n\nAnother risk: In some pygments versions, 'application/json' mimetype might map to JsonLexer only; yes.\n\nLet me also think about `test_mime_resolved_by_lexer_name` with 'foo/python': is there any lexer with mimetype 'foo/python'? No. Name 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer (pygments.lexers.python.PythonLexer). Good.\n\nHmm, wait: could `get_lexer_for_mimetype('foo/python')` accidentally match via the regex pattern quirk? Pattern `^\\*?(foo/python)$` matched against registered mimetypes \u2014 none is 'foo/python'. Good.\n\nNow, about cleanup: monkeypatch handles restoration automatically. No other state.\n\nAlso the instruction: \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 just imports and test functions. No pytest.main.\n\nLet me also consider using pytest.mark.parametrize to reduce repetition, but explicit tests are clearer for coverage mapping. I'll write individual tests, maybe a couple parametrized.\n\nLet me now call get_info to verify the function and its module.\n\nI'll call:\n- get_info('get_lexer')\n\nMaybe also get_info('pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype') and get_info('pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name') to confirm they exist as described. These are independent calls; I can batch them.\n\nAfter that, write the final test code.\n\nLet me draft the final test file:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nimport pygments.lexers\nimport pytest\nfrom pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer\nfrom pygments.util import ClassNotFound\n\nfrom httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer\n\n\ndef expected_lexer_class(name):\n    return type(pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name))\n\n\ndef test_mime_resolved_directly_by_mimetype():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/html')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('html')\n\n\ndef test_mime_resolved_by_lexer_name_after_mimetype_failure():\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/python')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('python')\n\n\ndef test_unknown_mime_returns_none():\n    assert get_lexer('foo/bar') is None\n\n\ndef test_plus_suffix_json_resolved_via_derived_mimetype(monkeypatch):\n    tried = []\n    real_get_lexer_for_mimetype = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype\n\n    def spy_get_lexer_for_mimetype(mimetype, **options):\n        tried.append(mimetype)\n        return real_get_lexer_for_mimetype(mimetype, **options)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\n        pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_for_mimetype', spy_get_lexer_for_mimetype\n    )\n\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+json')\n\n    assert tried == [\n        'application/vnd.foo+json',\n        'application/vnd.foo',\n        'application/json',\n    ]\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_plus_suffix_non_json_resolved_via_derived_mimetype():\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+xml')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('xml')\n\n\ndef test_plus_suffix_all_mimetypes_fail_falls_back_to_names(monkeypatch):\n    tried_names = []\n    real_get_lexer_by_name = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name\n\n    def spy_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options):\n        tried_names.append(name)\n        return real_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\n        pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_by_name', spy_get_lexer_by_name\n    )\n\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar+python')\n\n    assert tried_names == ['bar', 'python']\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('python')\n\n\ndef test_json_in_subtype_appends_json_lexer_name(monkeypatch):\n    tried_names = []\n    real_get_lexer_by_name = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name\n\n    def spy_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options):\n        tried_names.append(name)\n        return real_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\n        pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_by_name', spy_get_lexer_by_name\n    )\n\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar+json')\n\n    assert tried_names == ['bar', 'json', 'json']\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_mimetype_hit_skips_name_lookup(monkeypatch):\n    called = False\n    real_get_lexer_by_name = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name\n\n    def spy_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options):\n        nonlocal called\n        called = True\n        return real_get_lexer_by_name(name, **options)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\n        pygments.lexers, 'get_lexer_by_name', spy_get_lexer_by_name\n    )\n\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/json')\n\n    assert called is False\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_promotes_text_lexer_to_json():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert type(lexer) is expected_lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_invalid_body_keeps_text_lexer():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='not json')\n    assert isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_no_lexer_valid_body_promotes_