      "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze the code to understand what needs to be tested.\n\nThe function `get_lexer` takes a mime type, an `explicit_json` flag, and a body. It:\n\n1. Splits the mime into type and subtype\n2. If '+' not in subtype, appends subtype to lexer_names\n3. Else, splits subtype on '+' and extends lexer_names with both parts, and extends mime_types with the two combined types\n4. If 'json' in subtype, appends 'json' to lexer_names\n5. Tries to resolve lexer by mime type; if none found, tries by name\n6. If explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)), tries to parse body as JSON; if successful, uses JSON lexer\n7. Returns lexer\n\nLines 115-156 need coverage. Let me think about test cases:\n\n1. Basic mime type without '+': e.g., 'text/plain' \u2192 TextLexer via mime lookup\n2. Mime type with '+': e.g., 'application/vnd.api+json' \u2192 splits into subtype_name='vnd.api', subtype_suffix='json', mime_types extended\n3. 'json' in subtype: e.g., 'application/json' \u2192 lexer_names gets 'json' appended\n4. Mime type lookup fails, name lookup succeeds: e.g., 'foo/bar' where 'bar' is a valid lexer name\n5. Mime type lookup fails, name lookup fails: returns None\n6. explicit_json with valid JSON body and no lexer found: returns JSON lexer\n7. explicit_json with invalid JSON body: returns None (or the original lexer)\n8. explicit_json with valid JSON body and TextLexer: returns JSON lexer\n9. explicit_json with valid JSON body and non-TextLexer lexer: returns original lexer\n\nLet me check the actual httpie code to be sure. The actual code from httpie:\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer(\n    mime: str,\n    explicit_json=False,\n    body=''\n) -> Optional[Type[Lexer]]:\n    # Build candidate mime type and lexer names.\n    mime_types, lexer_names = [mime], []\n    type_, subtype = mime.split('/', 1)\n    if '+' not in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append(subtype)\n    else:\n        subtype_name, subtype_suffix = subtype.split('+', 1)\n        lexer_names.extend([subtype_name, subtype_suffix])\n        mime_types.extend([\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_name),\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_suffix)\n        ])\n\n    # As a last resort, if no lexer feels responsible, and\n    # the subtype contains 'json', take the JSON lexer\n    if 'json' in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append('json')\n\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n\n    if explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)):\n        # JSON response with an incorrect Content-Type?\n        try:\n            json.loads(body)  # FIXME: the body also gets parsed in json.py\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # Nope\n        else:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n\n    return lexer\n```\n\nWait, there's a subtlety with the for-else: the `else` clause of the for loop executes only if the loop completes without `break`. So if the first mime_type lookup succeeds, we break and skip the name lookup. If all mime_type lookups fail, the else clause runs the name lookups.\n\nNote: in the name lookup loop, there's no break \u2014 it keeps trying all names, and the last successful one wins. Actually wait, looking at the code:\n\n```python\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n```\n\nHmm, there's no break here in the shown code. Let me check the actual httpie source... Actually in the real httpie code:\n\n```python\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n```\n\nYes, that's the actual code \u2014 no break in the inner loop. So the last name that resolves wins. Interesting.\n\nNow, for testing, I need to know the module path. The code is from `httpie/output/formatters/colors.py`. So I'd import `from httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer`.\n\nLet me think about what pygments lexers are available:\n- 'text/plain' \u2192 TextLexer (via mimetype lookup, pygments maps text/plain to TextLexer)\n- 'application/json' \u2192 JsonLexer via mimetype\n- 'text/html' \u2192 HtmlLexer\n- 'application/xml' \u2192 XmlLexer\n- 'foo/bar' \u2192 no mimetype lexer; name 'bar' \u2192 probably ClassNotFound; returns None\n- 'application/vnd.api+json': subtype = 'vnd.api+json', subtype_name='vnd.api', subtype_suffix='json'. mime_types = ['application/vnd.api+json', 'application/vnd.api', 'application/json']. 'application/json' resolves to JsonLexer. lexer_names = ['vnd.api', 'json', 'json'] (since 'json' in subtype).\n\nHmm wait, for 'application/json': subtype = 'json', no '+', so lexer_names = ['json']. Then 'json' in subtype \u2192 append 'json' again \u2192 ['json', 'json']. mime_types = ['application/json'] which resolves via mimetype. So the name loop isn't reached.\n\nFor a case where mimetype lookup fails but name lookup succeeds: e.g., 'text/x-python'? Actually pygments maps 'text/x-python' to PythonLexer I think. Let me think of a mime type that pygments doesn't know but whose subtype is a lexer name. E.g., 'application/x-sh'? pygments knows 'application/x-sh' \u2192 BashLexer? Hmm.\n\nLet me think: 'foo/python' \u2014 mimetype lookup fails for 'foo/python', then name lookup for 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer. That works.\n\nFor a case where nothing resolves: 'foo/bar' \u2192 lexer_names = ['bar'], 'bar' is not a lexer name \u2192 returns None.\n\nFor the explicit_json branch:\n- explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}', mime='text/plain' \u2192 mimetype lookup gives TextLexer \u2192 isinstance(lexer, TextLexer) is True \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 lexer = JSON lexer.\n- explicit_json=True, body='not json', mime='text/plain' \u2192 TextLexer \u2192 json.loads raises ValueError \u2192 pass \u2192 returns TextLexer.\n- explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}', mime='application/json' \u2192 JsonLexer (not TextLexer) \u2192 condition `(not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer))` is False \u2192 returns JsonLexer without parsing. Hmm, but this doesn't cover the branch where explicit_json is True but the condition short-circuits... Actually for branch coverage, we want both outcomes of the `if` condition.\n- explicit_json=True, body='' \u2192 condition False due to empty body.\n- explicit_json=False, body='{\"a\": 1}', mime='text/plain' \u2192 returns TextLexer.\n\nAlso the case where lexer is None and explicit_json and body: 'foo/bar' with explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}' \u2192 lexer is None \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 JSON lexer.\n\nAnd 'foo/bar' with explicit_json=True, body='not json' \u2192 lexer None \u2192 json.loads fails \u2192 returns None.\n\nNow, about the '+' branch: 'application/vnd.api+json' covers lines 120-124. Also need a '+' case where 'json' is not in subtype? E.g., 'application/vnd.foo+xml' \u2192 subtype_name='vnd.foo', subtype_suffix='xml'. mime_types = ['application/vnd.foo+xml', 'application/vnd.foo', 'application/xml']. 'application/xml' resolves \u2192 XmlLexer. That covers the '+' branch without json.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 do I need to worry about pygments version differences? The tests should be robust. Let me use well-known mappings:\n- 'text/plain' \u2192 TextLexer (pygments maps 'text/plain' to TextLexer)\n- 'application/json' \u2192 JsonLexer\n- 'text/html' \u2192 HtmlLexer\n- 'application/xml' \u2192 XmlLexer\n\nThese are standard pygments mimetype mappings. Let me verify with get_info tool... Actually, the get_info tool returns information about a symbol in the codebase (class, function or method name). Let me use it to check `get_lexer` and maybe pygments lexers.\n\nActually, the tool description says \"Returns information about a symbol.\" \u2014 probably it can look up functions in the project. Let me call get_info on 'get_lexer' to see the actual source, and maybe on pygments lexers to verify names.\n\nLet me plan the calls:\n- get_info('get_lexer') \u2014 to see the full function including imports/context\n- get_info('pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype') \u2014 maybe not needed\n- Maybe check 'TextLexer', 'JsonLexer'\n\nI'll make independent calls in one block.\n\nNow, about cleanup/state pollution: the function is pure (no global state modification), so tests don't need much cleanup. But to be safe, we could monkeypatch nothing. Actually, there's no state to pollute. But the instructions say \"Be sure to clean up after the test... use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate.\" Since get_lexer is pure, no cleanup needed. But maybe I should avoid any patching. However, to be safe against pygments lexer registry changes... no, keep it simple.\n\nWait \u2014 one consideration: `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name` and `get_lexer_for_mimetype` may load lexers into a cache, but that's fine.\n\nLet me now write the tests:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\nfrom pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer\nfrom pygments.lexers.data import JsonLexer  # hmm, where is JsonLexer?\n```\n\nActually JsonLexer is in `pygments.lexers.data`. But I can avoid importing it by checking `lexer.name == 'JSON'` or using `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')` and comparing. Better: compare against the expected lexer instance type via `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json').__class__`.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer\nfrom pygments.util import ClassNotFound\n\nfrom httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer\n\n\ndef test_get_lexer_simple_mime_resolves_via_mimetype():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain')\n    assert isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)\n\n\ndef test_get_lexer_json_mime():\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/json')\n    json_lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n    assert type(lexer) is type(json_lexer)\n```\n\nHmm, actually `get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/json')` returns JsonLexer instance. Let me just assert `lexer.__class__.__name__ == 'JsonLexer'` or compare with get_lexer_by_name('json').\n\nLet me structure tests to cover all lines/branches:\n\n1. `test_simple_mime_found_by_mimetype`: `get_lexer('text/html')` \u2192 HtmlLexer. Covers lines 115-118, 133-137 (break), 156.\n\n2. `test_simple_mime_found_by_name`: `get_lexer('foo/python')` \u2192 mimetype lookup fails for 'foo/python' (ClassNotFound), then name 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer. Covers 138-139 (except pass), 141-143.\n\nWait \u2014 careful: does pygments know 'foo/python' as a mimetype? No. And name lookup 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer. Good. But note the loop doesn't break, so it continues; only one name here. Covers 141-145.\n\n3. `test_mime_not_found_returns_none`: `get_lexer('foo/bar')` \u2192 None. Covers 144-145 (except pass in name loop), 156.\n\n4. `test_subtype_with_plus_json`: `get_lexer('application/vnd.api+json')` \u2192 covers 120-124, 129-130. The mime_types list: ['application/vnd.api+json', 'application/vnd.api', 'application/json']. 'application/vnd.api+json' \u2014 does pygments know it? Probably not. 'application/vnd.api' \u2014 no. 'application/json' \u2014 yes \u2192 JsonLexer. So covers 134-138 loop with exceptions and break on third. Good.\n\nActually wait, does pygments know 'application/vnd.api+json'? Hmm, pygments might have some vendor mimetypes... I don't think vnd.api+json is there. But to be safe, maybe use something more obscure like 'application/vnd.foo+json'. Then mime_types = ['application/vnd.foo+json', 'application/vnd.foo', 'application/json'] \u2192 resolves on 'application/json'. lexer_names = ['vnd.foo', 'json', 'json'].\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety: pygments' `get_lexer_for_mimetype` \u2014 for 'application/vnd.foo+json', it won't match. OK.\n\n5. `test_subtype_with_plus_non_json`: `get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+xml')` \u2192 mime_types = ['application/vnd.foo+xml', 'application/vnd.foo', 'application/xml'] \u2192 'application/xml' resolves \u2192 XmlLexer. Covers '+' branch without 'json' in subtype (so line 129-130 branch False side).\n\n6. `test_explicit_json_valid_body_text_lexer`: `get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')` \u2192 TextLexer found, isinstance TextLexer \u2192 json.loads succeeds \u2192 JSON lexer. Covers 147, 149-151, 154.\n\n7. `test_explicit_json_invalid_body`: `get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='not json')` \u2192 ValueError \u2192 pass \u2192 returns TextLexer. Covers 151-152.\n\n8. `test_explicit_json_no_lexer_valid_body`: `get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')` \u2192 lexer None \u2192 JSON lexer. Covers `not lexer` side of condition.\n\n9. `test_explicit_json_no_lexer_invalid_body`: `get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='not json')` \u2192 returns None.\n\n10. `test_explicit_json_non_text_lexer_untouched`: `get_lexer('application/json', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')` \u2192 JsonLexer, not TextLexer \u2192 condition False \u2192 returns JsonLexer. Covers the False branch of 147.\n\n11. `test_explicit_json_empty_body`: `get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='')` \u2192 condition False (empty body) \u2192 TextLexer.\n\n12. `test_explicit_json_false`: `get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=False, body='{\"a\": 1}')` \u2192 TextLexer.\n\nAlso, for the for-else: the else branch (name lookup) only runs when no mimetype matched. Covered by tests 2, 3, 8, 9.\n\nAlso, the name loop with multiple names where an earlier name fails and later succeeds: e.g., 'foo/bar+baz' where... hmm. Let's see: 'application/vnd.foo+xml': lexer_names = ['vnd.foo', 'xml']. But mimetype lookup succeeds on 'application/xml', so name loop not reached. To exercise the name loop with multiple names, I need a mime where all mimetype lookups fail. E.g., 'foo/bar+python': subtype='bar+python', subtype_name='bar', subtype_suffix='python'. mime_types = ['foo/bar+python', 'foo/bar', 'foo/python'] \u2014 none known. lexer_names = ['bar', 'python', ...]. 'bar' fails, 'python' succeeds \u2192 PythonLexer. That covers the loop iterating with failures then success (no break, so it continues but only one success).\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle issue: since there's no break in the name loop, if 'bar' fails and 'python' succeeds, lexer = PythonLexer. Good.\n\nAlso a case where name loop has a success followed by failure? Since no break, a later failure doesn't reset lexer to None (except passes). So the last successful name wins. E.g., 'foo/python+json': subtype='python+json', names = ['python', 'json', 'json'] (json appended since 'json' in subtype). mime_types = ['foo/python+json', 'foo/python', 'foo/json'] \u2014 none known? 'foo/json' not known. So name loop: 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer, 'json' \u2192 JsonLexer, 'json' \u2192 JsonLexer. Final: JsonLexer. Hmm, that's interesting but maybe fragile. I'll skip that.\n\nNow, about verifying lexer classes: I'll use `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')` etc. to get expected classes, or import specific lexer classes:\n- `from pygments.lexers.html import HtmlLexer`\n- `from pygments.lexers.data import JsonLexer`\n- `from pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer`\n- `from pygments.lexers.python import PythonLexer`\n- `from pygments.lexers.html import XmlLexer` \u2014 actually XmlLexer is in pygments.lexers.html? Let me recall: `pygments.lexers.html` contains HtmlLexer, XmlLexer... Yes, XmlLexer is in pygments.lexers.html. Hmm, actually I think XmlLexer is in `pygments.lexers.html`. Let me double check: pygments.lexers.html has HtmlLexer, DtdLexer, XmlLexer, PhpLexer... Yes.\n\nBut to be safe across pygments versions, I could compare via `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)` and check `type(lexer) is type(expected)` or `isinstance`. Using get_lexer_by_name avoids import path issues.\n\nActually, simpler: assert `lexer.__class__ is pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json').__class__`. But calling get_lexer_by_name in the test is fine.\n\nAlternatively use `pygments.lexers.find_lexer_class_by_name`? Keep it simple.\n\nLet me also consider: does `get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/plain')` return TextLexer? In pygments, mimetype 'text/plain' maps to TextLexer. Yes.\n\n'text/html' \u2192 HtmlLexer. Yes.\n\n'application/xml' \u2192 XmlLexer. Yes.\n\n'application/json' \u2192 JsonLexer. Yes.\n\nNow, one concern: the environment might have a custom httpie ColorsFormatter etc. But we're only testing get_lexer.\n\nAlso, the module import: `from httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer`. The actual httpie code has this function. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nAlso note: the code snippet shows the function at lines 115-156, meaning it's inside a larger file. The import in the test should work.\n\nLet me also think about whether `get_lexer` might be affected by any global state \u2014 no.\n\nNow about \"clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution\" \u2014 since get_lexer is pure, there's nothing to clean. But maybe I can add a safety: no monkeypatching needed. However, the instructions emphasize cleanup; I could note that no state is modified. But maybe to be extra safe, I won't patch anything.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one thing: pygments lexers get instantiated and cached? `get_lexer_by_name` uses a lexers dict and instantiates; there's `_lexer_cache`. Loading lexers populates caches but that's benign.\n\nLet me now also double-check the branch coverage for line 117 `if '+' not in subtype:` \u2014 both branches covered (tests with and without '+').\n\nLine 129 `if 'json' in subtype:` \u2014 both branches covered ('application/json' \u2192 True; 'text/html' \u2192 False; 'application/vnd.foo+xml' \u2192 False).\n\nLine 134-140 for loop with break and else: covered.\n\nLine 141-145 name loop with success and failure: covered.\n\nLine 147 condition: covered True and False, including short-circuit variants:\n- `explicit_json` False \u2192 short-circuit (test 12)\n- `body` empty \u2192 short-circuit (test 11)\n- `not lexer` True (test 8)\n- `isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)` True (test 6)\n- both False (test 10)\n\nLine 149-152 try/except: covered both.\n\nLine 154: covered.\n\nLine 156 return: covered.\n\nNow let me write the test file. I should use pytest style, maybe parametrize. Let me write clean tests with assertions.\n\n```python\nimport pygments.lexers\nimport pytest\nfrom pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer\nfrom pygments.util import ClassNotFound\n\nfrom httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer\n\n\ndef _lexer_class(name):\n    return type(pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name))\n\n\ndef test_mime_resolved_directly_by_mimetype():\n    # Lines 115-118, 133-137 (loop + break), 156\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/html')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('html')\n\n\ndef test_mime_resolved_by_lexer_name():\n    # mimetype lookup fails -> name lookup succeeds (lines 138-145)\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/python')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('python')\n\n\ndef test_unknown_mime_returns_none():\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar')\n    assert lexer is None\n\n\ndef test_subtype_with_suffix_resolved_via_derived_mimetype():\n    # '+' branch (lines 120-124) with 'json' in subtype (129-130)\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+json')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_subtype_with_suffix_non_json():\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/vnd.foo+xml')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('xml')\n\n\ndef test_subtype_with_suffix_resolved_via_lexer_name():\n    # All mimetype candidates fail; name lookup walks candidates.\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar+python')\n    assert lexer is not None\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('python')\n\n\ndef test_json_mime_not_replaced_by_explicit_json():\n    lexer = get_lexer('application/json', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_promotes_text_lexer():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_invalid_body_keeps_text_lexer():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='not json')\n    assert isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_no_lexer_valid_body():\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert type(lexer) is _lexer_class('json')\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_no_lexer_invalid_body():\n    lexer = get_lexer('foo/bar', explicit_json=True, body='not json')\n    assert lexer is None\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_empty_body_no_promotion():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='')\n    assert isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)\n\n\ndef test_explicit_json_disabled_no_promotion():\n    lexer = get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=False, body='{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue with `test_subtype_with_suffix_resolved_via_lexer_name` using 'foo/bar+python': lexer_names = ['bar', 'python']. mime_types = ['foo/bar+python', 'foo/bar', 'foo/python']. Are any of these known mimetypes in pygments? 'foo/bar+python' no, 'foo/bar' no, 'foo/python' no. Good. Then name loop: 'bar' \u2192 ClassNotFound \u2192 pass; 'python' \u2192 PythonLexer. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: is there any chance pygments has a lexer named 'bar'? No.\n\nAlso 'foo/python' \u2014 mimetype lookup: pygments' get_lexer_for_mimetype iterates over all lexers checking mimetypes; 'foo/python' won't match. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: `test_unknown_mime_returns_none` \u2014 'foo/bar': name 'bar' \u2192 ClassNotFound \u2192 lexer stays None. Returns None. Good.\n\nNow, about `_lexer_class('html')` \u2014 get_lexer_by_name('html') \u2192 HtmlLexer. And get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/html') \u2192 HtmlLexer. Same class. Good.\n\nFor 'xml': get_lexer_by_name('xml') \u2192 XmlLexer. get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/xml') \u2192 XmlLexer. Good.\n\nFor 'json': JsonLexer. Good.\n\nFor 'python': PythonLexer. Good.\n\nNow, is there any risk that `get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/vnd.foo+json')` matches something? Pygments matches mimetypes by exact match or by suffix patterns? Let me think: `get_lexer_for_mimetype` iterates `find_lexer_class_for_mimetype`... Actually it does:\n\n```python\ndef find_lexer_class_for_mimetype(_mime):\n    for lexer in _iter_lexerclasses():\n        for mimetype in lexer.mimetypes:\n            if re.match(rf'^\\*?({_mime.replace(\"+\", r\"\\+\")})$', mimetype):\n                ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef find_lexer_class_for_filename(_fn):\n    ...\n```\n\nFor mimetype:\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer_for_mimetype(_mime, **options):\n    return get_lexer_by_mimetype... \n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef find_lexer_class_for_mimetype(_mime):\n    \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n    for lexer in _iter_lexerclasses():\n        for mimetype in lexer.mimetypes:\n            if re.match(rf'^\\*?({_mime.replace(\"+\", r\"\\+\")})$', mimetype):\n                return lexer\n    return None\n```\n\nWait, that regex matches if the *registered* mimetype matches the queried mime pattern... Actually 